## ABSTRACT
Polymer solar cells (PSCs) with high power conversion efficiency (PCE) and environment-friendly fabrication are the main requirements  enabling  their  production  in  industrial  scale.  While  the  use  of  non-halogenated  solvent  processing  is inevitable for the PSC fabrication, it significantly reduces the processability of polymer donors $(P_{\mathsf{D S}})$ and small-molecule acceptors (SMAs). This often results in unoptimized blend morphology and limits the device performance. To address this issue,  hydrophilic  oligoethylene  glycol  (OEG)  side-chains  are  introduced  into  a $P_{\mathsf{D}}$ (2EG)  to  enhance  the  molecular compatibility between the $P_{\mathsf{D}}$ and L8-BO SMA. The 2EG $P_{\mathsf{D}}$ induces higher crystallinity and alleviates phase separation with the SMA compared to the reference $P_{\mathsf{D}}$ (PM7) with hydrocarbon side-chains. Consequently, the 2EG-based PSCs exhibit  a  higher  PCE $(15.8\%)$ than  the  PM7-based  PSCs $(\mathsf{P C E}=14.4\%)$ in  the  ortho-xylene  based  processing. Importantly, benefitted from the reduced phase separation and increased crystallinity of 2EG $P_{\mathsf{D}\mathsf{S}}$ , the 2EG-based PSCs show enhanced thermal stability $84\%$ of initial PCE after $120{\mathsf{h}}$ heating) compared to that of the PM7-based PSCs $(60\%$ of  initial  PCE  after $120\ h$ heating).  This  study  demonstrates  the  potential  of  OEG  side-chain-incorporated  materials  in developing efficient, stable, and eco-friendly PSCs.
## KEYWORDS
polymer solar cell, polymer donor, OEG side-chain, non-halogenated solvent process, side-chain engineering
## 1    Introduction
Polymer  solar  cells  (PSCs)  are  promising  candidates  as  power suppliers  for  wearable  devices  due  to  their  lightweight  and mechanical flexibility [1–3]. The development of various polymer donors $(P_{\mathrm{DS}})$ and small molecule acceptors (SMAs) has improved the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of PSCs to above $18\%-19\%$ [4–19]. However, the use of hazardous processing solvents during fabrication  has  hindered  the  large-scale  production  and commercialization  of  PSCs.  Most  high-performance  PSC  blends involve the solution processing based on halogenated solvents (i.e., chloroform  (CF)  or  chlorobenzene)  for  their  fabrication,  which are extremely hazardous [20–23]. For example, lethal dose (LD50) values of these solvents are only less than $2~\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1};$ , which indicates a sufficient amount to kill $50\%$ of rats during the oral ingestion of that  solvent  [24, 25].  To  overcome  this  issue,  it  is  imperative  to develop  new  active  layer  materials  that  can  achieve  high performance  PSCs  without  involving  the  process  using halogenated solvents [26–30].
The solubilities of efficient $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ and SMAs in non-halogenated solvents such as toluene, and ortho-xylene $\stackrel{\cdot}{o}$ -xylene) are generally much  lower  than  those  in  halogenated  solvents,  demanding  a solution  processing  at  elevated  temperatures  for  suitable  film formation [24, 26, 31]. In order to apply sufficient heat during the process,  non-halogenated  solvents  used  for  PSC  fabrication typically have higher bp (e.g., toluene $(110~\mathrm{^{\circ}C})$ , and $o$ -xylene (144 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ) than  CF  (61 $\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C}$ ),  the  most  common  halogenated  solvent. Insufficient solubility and elongated processing time for the film formation by high boiling point solvents induce accelerated liquidliquid  phase  separation  [id  phase  separation  [ation.  Insufficient solubility and elongated processing time for the film formation by high boiling point solvents induce accelerated liquid-liquid phase separation  [32–35]  and,  thus,  often  results  in  excessive  phase separation. This unoptimized blend morphology can impoverish charge generation and transport properties of the PSCs [36–38].
To  this  end,  the  design  of $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ that  tune  their  aggregation structures  as  well  as  improve  their  molecular  interaction  with SMAs should be considered. For example, the reduced molecular demixing  between  the $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ and  SMAs  can  improve  the  blend morphology by facilitating the formation of sufficient intermixed domains  [31, 35].  These  molecular  interactions  between  the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ and SMAs are highly affected by the discrepancies between their hydrophilicity  and  surface  tension  values.  When  the  surface tension  values  of  the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ and  SMA  are  similar,  their  molecular interactions are expected to be more favorable. In general, SMA molecules show higher polarity and hydrophilicity than $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ due to the  presence  of  a  malononitrile  group  in  SMA  backbone  [39]. Therefore,  SMA-coated  films  exhibit  lower  contact  angle  with hydrophilic  solvent  (i.e.,  water  and  glycerol)  and  higher  surface tension values compared to those of the $P_{\mathrm{DS}}.$ . Thus, new types of $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ with higher hydrophilicity can possess low interfacial tension values with SMAs, and prevent the severe phase separation during the film formation.
To enhance the hydrophilicity of $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ and reduce the molecular demixing between the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ and SMA, side-chain engineering can be a plausible avenue. Specifically, the hydrophilicity of polymers can be  easily  controlled  by  adjusting  chemical  elements,  branching types,  and  lengths  of  side-chains  [31, 40–43].  Among  numerous side-chains, oligoethylene glycol (OEG)-based side-chains are an attractive platform for enhancing the hydrophilicity of a $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ due to the  oxygen  atoms  in  the  OEG  group  [44–47].  For  instance, Wang’s  group  developed  an  isoindigo-based $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ (P-OEG)  by attaching branched OEG side-chains [48]. The surface energy of POEG $\left(30.8~\mathrm{mJ}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\right)$ was closer to the acceptor $\left(34.2~\mathrm{mJ}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\right)$ ) than that of P-Alkyl $\left(26.5\mathrm{mJ}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\right)$ , the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ containing hydrocarbon sidechains. As the P-OEG-based blend showed more alleviated phase separation  than  the  P-Alkyl-based  blend,  P-OEG-based  PSCs $(\mathrm{PCE}=5.1\%$ )  achieved  higher  performance  than  P-Alkyl  based PSCs $\mathrm{^{\prime}P C E}=2.3\%\$ ). As another example, You’s group developed 5 different OEG side-chain incorporated benzodithiophene (BDT)- based $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ (P2-P6) by adjusting the side-chain position and their length  [45].  They  compared  the  photovoltaic  performance  and blend  morphology  of  the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ :IT-M-based  PSCs  in  the  toluenebased  and  2-methyltetrahydrofuran-based  processing.  They reported that a proper OEG side-chain incorporated $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ (P2) can achieve  a  higher  PCE $(7.1\%)$ than  the  other $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ .  As  another example of using OEG groups in the $P_{\mathrm{D}},$ Kim’s group introduced OEG spacers along the backbone of PM6 to reduce the interfacial tension $(\gamma^{\mathrm{{D-A}}})$ between  PM6  and  SMA  (BTP-eC9)  [45].  PM6- OEG, the developed $P_{\mathrm{D}},$ , showed favorable interaction with SMA, resulting  in  well-mixed  blend  morphology  in  the  toluene-based fabrication  process.  Hence,  PM6-OEG-based  PSCs  exhibited  a higher PCE $(17.7\%)$ than PM6-based PSCs $\mathrm{(PCE}=15.6\%\$ ). From the combined results, OEG-incorporated materials can be suitable candidates for favorable $P_{\mathrm{D}}{:}\mathrm{SMA}$ blend morphology by reducing the interfacial tension between the components of active layer.
In this work, we design a series of BDT-based $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ featuring a linear  OEG  side-chain  (1-ethoxy-2-methoxyethane)  or  branched hydrocarbon  (2-ethylhexyl,  EH)  to  elucidate  the  effect  of  sidechain engineering on blend morphology and performance of nonhalogenated solvent-processed PSCs. According to the portion of side-chain at BDT unit, the $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ were denoted as PM7 ( $100\%$ EH) [49], $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}$ $50\%$ EH  and $50\%$ OEG),  and  2EG  ( $100\%$ OEG).  The  2EG $P_{\mathrm{D}},$ in  which  all  side-chains  at  BDT  are  linear OEG,  possessed  the  strongest  crystallinity  and  lowest  interfacial tension with L8-BO SMA $(\gamma^{\mathrm{{D-A}}}$ of PM7:L8- $\mathrm{BO}=0.8\ \mathrm{mN}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ and $\gamma^{\mathrm{{D-A}}}$ of 2EG:L8- $\mathrm{\cdotBO}{=}0.1\ \mathrm{mN\cdotm^{-1}}$ ). As a result, the PCE of $o$ -xyleneprocessed PSC based on 2EG:L8-BO $(15.8\%)$ was higher than that based  on  PM7:L8-BO $(14.4\%)$ owing  to  suppressed  phase separation. Furthermore, the thermal stability of 2EG-based PSC $84\%$ of  initial  PCE  after $120\mathrm{~h~}$ of  heating  at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ was significantly  higher  than  that  of  PM7-based  PSC $60\%$ of  initial PCE after $120\mathrm{h}$ of heating at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ ). These findings demonstrate that polymers with hydrophilic OEG side-chains can enhance the miscibility with SMA, improve the PCE and device stability of the PSCs in the non-halogenated processing.
## 2    Results and discussion
## 2.1    Design, synthesis and characterization of materials
## 2.2    Aggregation,  crystalline,  and  hydrophilic  properties of thin films
To  investigate  the  influence  of  OEG  side-chains  on $P_{\mathrm{D}}{}^{\mathrm{~,~}}$ ’s aggregation and crystalline properties, we compared the UV‒vis, grazing incidence wide angle X-ray scattering (GIXS), and space charge limited current (SCLC) of pristine $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ films. In the film UVvis spectra (Fig. 2(a)), the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ with OEG side-chain showed higher intermolecular interaction than the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ with EH hydrocarbon sidechain. For example, the PM7 film showed a blue-shifted spectrum $(\lambda_{\mathrm{{max}}}=603~\mathrm{{nm})}$ compared to the other $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ and similar (0-0) and (0-1)  peak  intensities $(I_{0-0}/I_{0-1}=1.02)$ .  In  contrast,  the $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ with
OEG  side-chains  exhibited  much  red-shifted  spectra $(\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}\mathrm{=}615$ nm  for $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}$ and $621~\mathrm{\nm}$ for  2EG)  and  enhanced aggregation $(I_{0-0}/I_{0-1}=1.06$ for $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}$ and  1.05  for  2EG). This finding suggests that the increased intermolecular interaction observed for 2EG $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ in solution was effectively translated to the film state [53].
We examined the crystalline properties and hole mobility of the neat $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ films using GIXS and SCLC methods. All $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ displayed a face-on  preferred  packing  structure,  which  was  characterized  by distinct (010) peak along the out-of-plane (OOP) direction (Fig. 2(b) and Fig. S8  in  the  ESM).  For  quantitative  analysis,  we calculated stacking distance $(d)$ and crystal coherence length $(L_{\mathrm{c}})$ of (010) peaks using the Scherrer equation [54]. The $d$ value of (010)  peak  decreased  as  OEG  side-chain  was  attached  to $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ instead of EH side-chain. For instance, $d_{010}$ value decreased from 3.85  Å  (PM7)  to  3.82  Å $(\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5})$ ,  and  further  to $3.76\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (2EG). Additionally, increased $L_{\mathrm{C(010)}}$ values in the order of PM7 $(15.1\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})<\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}\dot{-}2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}$ $(15.5~\mathrm{\AA})<2\mathrm{EG}$ $(18.7\mathrm{~\AA~})$ indicate  that introducing  OEG  side-chains  into $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ promotes  relatively  larger crystallite  sizes  compared  to  EH  side-chain.  The  less  steric hindrance  of  OEG  side-chains  during  film  formation  can contribute to the tight packing of 2EG, as compared to PM7 [44].
We further investigated the hydrophilic properties of $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ and molecular compatibility between the $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ and L8-BO SMA using a contact-angle measurement (Fig. S9 and Table S1 in the ESM) [57, 58].  The  presence  of  OEG  side-chains  in  the $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ affords  higher hydrophilicity,  as  supported  by  lower  contact  angles  with  water and glycerol droplets, compared to $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ with EH side-chains. For example, the contact angles between water droplets and neat $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ film decreased in the order of PM7 $(103.5^{\circ})$ , $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}\:(\Omega$ 6.3°), and 2EG $(90.3^{\circ})$ . Thus, the surface tension value of $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ increased from PM7 $\mathrm{^{19.6\mN{\cdot}m^{-1}}}$ ), $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}$ $(21.9\ \mathrm{mN\cdotm^{-1}})$ ), and 2EG $(26.9\mathrm{\mN{\cdot}m^{-1}})$ ).  The  surface  tension  of  2EG  showed  the  closer value to L8-BO SMA $(24.9~\mathrm{mN}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-1})$ ) compared to other $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ . The interfacial tension $(\gamma^{\mathrm{{D-A}}})$ values between the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ and L8-BO SMA were  estimated  from  Wu’s  model  [57].  As  OEG  side-chain  was introduced  into  the $P_{\mathrm{D}},\gamma^{\mathrm{D-A}}$ values  decreased.  Specifically, $\gamma^{\mathrm{{D-A}}}$ values  of  PM7, $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}$ and  2EG  with  L8-BO  SMA  were 0.79, 0.57 and $0.14~\mathrm{mN}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ , respectively. This result suggests that the introduction of OEG side-chain in $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ can effectively control the surface tension of $P_{\mathrm{DS}},$ resulting in decreased interfacial tension between $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ and  SMA.  This  improved  molecular  compatibility between $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ and SMA may reduce the mixing enthalpy, thereby promoting favorable blend morphology during film formation in
$o$ -xylene [59, 60].
## 2.3    Photovoltaic  performance  of  PSCs  and  electrical properties
To investigate the impact of OEG side-chains on the performance of  PSCs,  we  fabricated  conventional-typed  PSCs  with  L8-BO SMA.  The  details  of  PSC  fabrication  are  described  in  the Experimental Section. As summarized in Fig. 3 and Table 3, 2EGbased  PSC  achieved  the  highest  PCE  of $15.80\%$ ,  which outperformed  the  PM7- $(14.35\%)$ and $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}$ - $(14.80\%)$ based PSCs. The short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ values of PSCs showed an increase in the order of PM7:L8-BO $(22.40\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})<$ $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}\mathrm{.L}8–\mathrm{BO}$ $(23.51\mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}})$ $\angle{\mathbf{\xi}}<\mathbf{\zeta}$ 2EG:L8-BO  (24.60 $\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ . The fill factor (FF) values also increased from PM7:L8- BO $(0.69)<\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}\mathrm{:}\mathrm{L}8\mathrm{-}\mathrm{BC}$ $(0.70)<21$ EG:L8-BO (0.73). The lower open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ value of 2EG-(0.86 V) and $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}.$ - $2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}$ -based (0.88 V) PSCs than that of PM7-based PSC (0.91 V) was  attributed  to  the  upshifted  HOMO  energy  levels  of  2EGincorporated $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ .  Notably,  2EG-based  PSC  exhibited  a  higher PCE  than  PM7-based  PSC  even  when  processed  with  other solvents (i.e., CF and toluene), as shown in Table S2 in the ESM. As  shown  in  the Fig. 3(c),  2EG:L8-BO  blend  showed  higher external quantum efficiency (EQE) values in both the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ (550‒650 nm)  and  SMA  range $(700-850\ \mathrm{nm})$ )  absorption  ranges  than  the other blends. All calculated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values showed similar values with $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values from PSCs within $2\%$ error range. Additionally, the EQE spectra were well matched with absorption ranges of the $P_{\mathrm{D}}{:}\mathrm{L}8-$ BO blend films (Fig. S10 in the ESM).
To elucidate the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ difference between the PSCs, photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{ph}})$ as a function of effective voltage $\cdot V_{\mathrm{eff}})$ was measured (Fig. 3(d)).  The $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values  have  close  correlation  with  exciton dissociation properties $(P(E,T))$ value from $V_{\mathrm{eff}}^{}-J_{\mathrm{ph}},$ as $P(E,T)$ value indicates the charge generation of PSC [61]. The $P(E,T)$ value of 2EG:L8-BO PSC $(95\%)$ was higher than other systems $(90.8\%$ for PM7- and $92.3\%$ for $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}$ -based  PSCs).  This  suggests that charge generation in 2EG-based blend is more effecient than PM7-based blend. Photoluminescence (PL) spectra of pristine L8- BO  and  blend  films  were  measured  to  evaluate  charge  transfer capability (Fig. S11 in the ESM, excitation wavelength $=633~\mathrm{nm}$ ). The quenching efficiency values increased in the order of PM7:L8-
BO blend $(89.7\%)<\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}-2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}\mathrm{:}\mathrm{L}8\mathrm{-}\mathrm{BO}$ blend $(92.5\%)<2\mathrm{EG}.\mathrm{L}8-$ BO blend $(94.3\%)$ . This result supports the highest $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of 2EG:L8- BO blend among the all systems from the notable charge transfer capability from L8-BO to $2\mathrm{EG}P_{\mathrm{D}}$ .
We  performed  further  investigation  on  the  relationship between light intensity $(P_{\mathrm{light}})$ and $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of PSCs to investigate their charge  recombination  properties  (Fig. S12(a)  in  the  ESM)  [62]. The  intensity  of  monomolecular  recombination  can  be determined by the slope S (unit: $k T{\cdot}q^{-1}.$ , where $k$ is the Boltzmann constant, $T$ is the temperature, and $q$ is the elementary charge) of the log-linear plot $(P_{\mathrm{light}}-V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ . A relatively lower S value indicates less  bimolecular  recombination  at  open  circuit.  The  2EG:L8-BO blend  showed  a  lower S value  of  1.10  compared  to $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}.$ - $2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}{:}\mathrm{L}8$ -BO (1.14) and PM7:L8-BO (1.17) blends, indicating that the  monomolecular  recombination  is  most  suppressed  in  the 2EG:L8-BO PSC. Additionally, the bimolecular recombination of each PSC was compared by comparing the slope $\alpha$ at the log-log plot $(P_{\mathrm{light}}{-}J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ (Fig. S10(b) in the ESM). All PSCs showed similar $\alpha$ values  (0.82−0.84),  indicating  similar  bimolecular  recombination at short circuit. The $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ and electron mobility $(\mu_{\mathrm{e}})$ of each blend film were measured by SCLC method (Table S3 in the ESM). The $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}s$ of blend films showed the similar trend to that of the neat $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ films.  The $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ values  of  PM7, $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5},$ and  2EG $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ -based blend  were $2.1\times10^{-4}$ , $4.0~\times~10^{-4}$ ,  and $5.5\ \times\ 10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm^{2}{\cdot}V^{-1}{\cdot}s^{-1}},$ , respectively. The balance between the hole mobility and electron mobility $(\mu_{\mathrm{h}}/\mu_{\mathrm{e}})$ values of PM7, $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}.$ , and 2EG $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ -based blend  were  0.45,  0.89,  and  1.15,  respectively.  The  most  highest hole mobility and well-balanced mobility ratio of 2EG-based blend supports its the highest FF value [55].
## 2.4    Morphological properties of blend films
We analyzed the morphological characteristics of blend films by performing the GIXS, resonant soft X-ray scattering (RSoXS), and in  situ UV‒vis  spectroscopy  measurements  of $P_{\mathrm{D}}{:}\mathrm{L}8{-}\mathrm{BO}$ blend films.  All  blends  showed  face-on  preferred  packing  structure represented by distinct (010) peaks along the OOP direction in the blend  GIXS  profiles  (Fig. 4(a) and Fig. S13  in  the  ESM).  The $L_{\mathrm{c}}$ values of the OOP (010) peak increased with increasing contents of the OEG side-chain in the $P_{\mathrm{DS}}.$ . For example, the $L_{\mathrm{c}}$ (010) values of PM7, $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5},$ ­and 2EG-based blend were 10.8, 17.3, and $21.7\mathrm{~\AA},$ respectively,  which  supports  enhanced  charge  transport properties in the 2EG-based blend. Next, we investigated RSoXS profiles  of  PM7:L8-BO  and  2EG:L8-BO  blend  films  to  compare their  domain  size  and  purity.  The  2EG:L8-BO  film  exhibited significantly lower domain size $(23\mathrm{nm})$ ) than the PM7:L8-BO film $\cdot106~\mathrm{~nm})$ .  Furthermore,  the  relative  domain  purity  (√TSI), estimated by the total scattering intensity of the RSoXS profile, of 2EG:L8-BO  film  (0.61)  was  significantly  lower  than  that  of PM7:L8-BO  film  (1.00).  Therefore,  the  phase  separation  in $P_{\mathrm{D}}.$ - SMA domains was suppressed by the incorporation of OEG sidechains into the $P_{\mathrm{DS}},$ resulting in the formation of relatively small and  well-intermixed  domains.  The  small  domain  size $(\sim23\ \mathrm{nm})$ and well-mixed blend morphology are advantageous for efficient charge generation and transport, which support the higher PCE of 2EG-based blend than PM7-based blend [56, 63–65].
This distinct difference in the blend morphology could be also attributed  to  the  changes  in  the  kinetic  process  for  the  film formation due to different aggregation structures of the $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ [31, $59,66,67]$ .  As $o$ -xylene  exhibits  a  higher  boiling  point $(144~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ compared to other solvents (i.e., CF $(61^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and toluene $\left(110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)_{\cdot}$ ), the film formation process with $o$ -xylene takes longer than other solvents. Thus, we compared the solidification process of $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ and L8-BO SMA in $P_{\mathrm{D}}{:}\mathrm{L}8{-}\mathrm{BO}$ blend. The saturation time $(t_{\mathrm{sat}})$ of $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ and L8-BO was estimated using intensity profiles at $600{-}615~\mathrm{nm}$ $(P_{\mathrm{DS}})$ and $790~\mathrm{nm}$ (L8-BO),  corresponding  to  the  absorption profiles of $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ and L8-BO. The $t_{\mathrm{sat}}s$ of PM7, $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5},$ and 2EG during the film formation were found to be 15.3, 13.5, and $^{12.3\ s,}$ respectively. This result indicates that the introduction of linear OEG side-chain into the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ can accelerate the solidification process.  Additionally,  the $t_{\mathrm{sat}}s$ of  L8-BO  in  the  blends  decreased with increasing the OEG side-chain in the $P_{\mathrm{DS}}.$ PM7 (15.4 s) to $\mathrm{PM}7_{0.5}–2\mathrm{EG}_{0.5}$ (14.1  s),  and  2EG  (12.7  s).  We  speculate  that  the faster  solidification  observed  in  2EG-based  mixing  than  PM7- based mixing occured due to the higher crystalline property and lower solubility of 2EG $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ $\mathrm{15\mg{mL^{-1}}}$ in the $o$ -xylene) compare to  PM7 $25~\mathrm{mg{\cdot}m L^{-1}}$ in  the $o$ -xylene)  [59].  The  introduction  of linear  OEG  side-chain  instead  of  hydrocarbon  EH  side-chain promoted  the  faster  precipitation  of  liquid  state  during  the  film formation, which suppressed the development of excessively phaseseparated  morphology.  Consequently,  the  2EG-based  blend exhibited  the  blend  morphology  with  smaller  domain  size  and purity  compared  to  that  of  the  PM7-based  blend,  resulting  in enhanced charge generation and transport behaviors.
## 2.5    Thermal stability of PSCs
Next,  we  examined  the  thermal  stability  of  PSCs,  which  is  an important requirement for industrialization [68–70]. Specifically, we compared the PCE difference between PM7-based and 2EGbased PSCs under heating conditions of $120{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ The normalized PCEs of PSCs and their photovoltaic parameters are described in the Fig. 5(a) and  Table  S4  in  the  ESM,  respectively.  The  2EGbased PSCs exhibited greater stability than PM7-based PSCs. For example,  after $^{120\mathrm{~h~}}$ of  heating,  PM7-based  blend  showed  only $60\%$ of  its  initial  PCE $(8.55\%)$ ,  whereas  the  2EG-based  blend retained $84\%$ of  its  initial  PCE $(13.25\%)$ .  To  understand  this discrepancy in the thermal stability of the PSCs, we investigated the  impact  of  continuous  heating  on  the $P_{\mathrm{D}}{:}\mathrm{L}8{-}\mathrm{BO}$ blend morphology  by  analyzing  the  atomic  force  microscopy  (AFM) images of each blend (Fig. $5(\mathrm{b})_{.}^{.}$ ). The 2EG-based blend maintained a  consistent  blend  morphology  regardless  of  the  thermal  stress, whereas PM7-based blend showed significant irregularities on the film surface after heating. The root-mean-squre roughness $(R_{\mathrm{q}})$ of 2EG:L8-BO  blend  remained  nearly  unchanged $\mathrm{7.7~nm}$ for $^{0\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ for $120\mathrm{{h}}{\mathrm{{}}}$ ) under continuous heating, while the $R_{\mathrm{q}}$ of PM7:L8-BO significantly increased ( $\mathrm{.1.0\nm}$ for $0\mathrm{{h}}$ and $2.9\mathrm{nm}$ for $120\mathrm{{h}}\dot{}$ ) under the same heating condition. The irregular topology of  PM7-based  blend  caused  by  continuous  heating  can  have  a negative impact on charge generation and transport, resulting in a decrease  in  PCE  [71, 72].  The  improved  thermal  stability  of  the 2EG-based  PSCs  can  be  attributed  to  two  reasons.  Firstly,  the higher crystalline properties of the 2EG $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ compared to the PM7 $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ lead  to  a  more  ordered  and  crystalline  domain  in  the  blend morphology,  which  slows  down  the  diffusion  of  L8-BO  SMA during  the  heating  [73, 74].  Secondly,  the  well-intermixed  blend morphology  of  the  2EG:L8-BO  blend  is  more  favorable  for maintaining the original morphologies with large interfacial areas against the phase-separation by the thermal stress [36, 75].
Overall, introducing a hydrophilic OEG side-chain into the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ effectively  improves  the  molecular  compatibility  between  the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ and  SMA.  This  results  in  decreased  mixing  enthalpy  and  fast solidification during the film formation. As a result, the 2EG:L8- BO blend exhibited the $P_{\mathrm{D}}{:}\mathrm{SMA}$ blend morphology with reduced domain  size  and  purity  compared  to  that  of  the  PM7:L8-BO blend, leading to improved photovoltaic properties in the 2EG:L8- BO  PSC.  Furthermore,  the  enhanced  crystalline  property  and molecular compatibility of 2EG $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ contributed to the improved thermal stability of the PSC device.
## 3    Conclusions
In this study, we developed a 2EG $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ with a hydrophilic OEG sidechain and achieved efficient and thermally stable PSCs using nonhalogenated solvent ( $o$ -xylene) based processing. The incorporation  of  the  hydrophilic  OEG  side-chain  into  the $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ improved  the  crystallinity,  hydrophilicity,  and  molecular compatibility  with  L8-BO  SMA.  Thus,  the  2EG $P_{\mathrm{D}}{:}\mathrm{L}8{-}\mathrm{BO}$ had improved blend morphology with reduced domain size and more strongly-developed  crystalline  structures  compared  to  the  PM7 $P_{\mathrm{D}}{:}\mathrm{L}8{-}\mathrm{BO}$ blend.  As  a  result,  the  higher  PCE $(15.8\%)$ and enhanced thermal stability $84\%$ of initial PCE after $120\mathrm{h}$ heating) were  achieved  in  the  2EG-based  PSC  than  the  PM7-based  PSC $(\mathrm{PCE}=14.3\%$ and $60\%$ of initial PCE after $120\mathrm{~h~}$ heating). The morphological investigations, including GIXS, RSoXS, and in situ UV-vis absorptions were performed to elucidate the impact of the 2EG $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ on the blend morphology. Our results provide a useful guideline for designing $P_{\mathrm{DS}}$ to produce efficient and stable PSCs using non-halogenated solvent processing.
